  and for giving us the privilege of  remembering our distinguished alumna  bianca maria fincicontini  the fincey contini lecture sponsors a  distinguished speaker in the field of  comparative literature  broadly defined over the years the  whitney has welcomed  translators editors theorists and  practitioners of literature  the inaugural lecture in the series was  delivered in 1991  by the great italian semiotician umberto  ecco  this year for the first time the whitney  humanities center  is joining with the yale review as  co-sponsors of the fincey cantini  lecture  it's my great pleasure now to introduce  the editor of the yale review megan  o'rourke  who took over the editorship of the yell  review in early 2020  as the magazine was celebrating its  200th  anniversary a graduate of yale  megan is an accomplished poet and  essayist  a former editor at the new yorker the  paris review and slate  she's the author of the best-selling  memoir the long goodbye  and three poetry collections she's  published essays on the love poems of  thomas hardy  on guantanamo on john ashbury  and on the early history of young adult  detective fiction  she has spent this pandemic year  finishing her next book of nonfiction  on appropriately enough illness  she's the recipient of many literary  awards including a guggenheim alanna  fellowship  and two pushcart prizes so megan i'm  passing the floor or the square  to you to introduce hernan diaz  thank you i'm sorry i i clicked on too  early but all the nicer to share the  screen  with you alice good afternoon everyone  i am extremely pleased to welcome hernan  diaz to give the finsi contini lecture  today  um at the whitney humanities center and  i want to thank alice kaplan and the  center for inviting the yale review to  be a co-sponsor  of this event we're very excited to say  that we'll be publishing  um hernan's lecture in print  in our summer issue which i'll remind  you of at the end so  we hope you'll subscribe or get a copy