 i think it's crucial  but you know it's not research in the  traditional sense  because i am i am not out to corroborate  some contextual facts or or trying to  make my prose more accurate  to give you like very quick examples the  the kind of stuff that i'm looking for  is very weird  i'm looking uh for instance for a  little little um how can i put it  little verbal props you know for example  in the case of the 19th century  uh that i think will do a lot more work  than really figuring out what happened  truly blow by blow at the whatever  mountain meadows massacre which is  important to know  but i think if you if you find that that  little word  um that will that will uh almost uh  alchemically conjure up a  whole a whole universe that is worth  more than than all the fact checking in  the world  and i get to those words by just  reading a lot while i'm writing i know a  lot of writer friends don't  even read fiction as they write the  wrong fiction i'm  the opposite uh like i read all of  dickens while writing in the distance  and that  somehow gave a sense of 19th century  language that that you can only get that  way just by being immersed in that world  and i don't think that's necessarily  research  no i love this distinction it's very  useful to me personally um  i'm going to ask one question from the  audience and then that's probably all we  have time for but  sorry um a member um no we wanted to  hear your wonderful talk um we had a  member of the audience ask  what advice do you have for authors who  are setting stories  even partially outside their own  cultural experience  in nations they don't know or among  people whose identity is the author  doesn't  hold her well yeah that's a really  complicated question  i think uh and it's something that i  that i deal with  uh myself i don't i don't i don't find  that i write  necessarily about myself and my  environment  i think the i think the first question  is why do you need to tell this story  i think i think that's the question that  uh you need to ask yourself  and and be dead honest with yourself  about it why  do you need to uh go into this world  when it when it it's not  given to you uh and i think the the  second element is  is never to be a tourist in in in  in that world you know uh and and to  really uh uh uh try to try to understand  it and if if you understand  why you need to go there chances are  that  maybe you will also understand uh uh uh  why you were drawn to it in the first  place but i think  it's a longer answer of course and and i  understand it has  a lot of political connotations but i  think we should all be brave enough  to venture outside our world and  and write about others because otherwise  you know literature  will be reduced to a selfie in a way  i love that distinction of the tourists  i do want to get to one more question if  you're patient enough  um hernan to do one more but of course  calabrese who is here  did ask a question i'd love to hear the  answer to and he said  as you were talking i kept thinking  about my reaction at seeing a work of  visual art and how similar that was  do you feel there is a is there a  difference excuse me can you repeat that  if  there's a oh it's here okay uh  oh yes no absolutely i think i think  it's  uh uh and and and thank you judge  calabrese for being here by the way it's  a it's it's a great honor i didn't know  you you were in the audience  and for making this this series possible  uh  definitely i think it's very important  uh to have  almost like a synesthetic approach to  literature  i think uh visual vividness is  all important but i'm also very  interested in  uh the auditive dimension uh of of of  prose and  and and how evocative sounds may be i  think literature gives us this  opportunity of and  and you megan as a poet you know  know all about this but just the way a a  word  looks and sounds and how it interacts  with its meaning is just such a  beautiful thing to  to use to one's advantage well this has  been  really wonderful thank you so much thank  you all for coming today  i know we're sad not to be in a physical  space together but we're really glad to  be in this virtual space together  delighted again to tell you the talk  will be published in the summer  issue of the yale review you can  subscribe at our website or pre-order  the issue if you're interested  and thank you again to alice kaplan and  the whitney humanities center for  hosting this event and having us all  here  thank you everyone have a wonderful day  thank you megan thank you everyone
 

